    <instruction>57–58 Intersection and Collision If two objects travel through space along two different curves, it’s often important to know whether they will collide. (Will a missile hit its moving target? Will two aircraft collide?) Their paths might intersect, but we need to know whether the objects are in the same position at the same time. (See Exercises 10.1.55, 10.1.56, and 10.1.57.)</instruction>
    <exercise number="57" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The trajectories of two particles are given by the vector functions</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$${\mathbf{r}_{1}(t) = {\langle{t^{2},7t - 12,t^{2}}\rangle}} {\mathbf{r}_{2}(t) = {\langle{4t - 3,t^{2},5t - 6}\rangle}}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>for $$t \geqslant 0$$ . Do the particles collide?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Yes</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
