    <exercise number="60" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Trefoil Knot The view of the trefoil knot shown in Figure 9 is accurate, but it doesn’t reveal the whole story. Use the parametric equations</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{rcl} x &amp; = &amp; \left( 2 + \cos 1.5t)\cos t \right. \\ y &amp; = &amp; \left( 2 + \cos 1.5t)\sin t \right. \\ z &amp; = &amp; {\sin 1.5t} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>to sketch the curve by hand as viewed from above, with gaps indicating where the curve passes over itself. Start by showing that the projection of the curve onto the $$xy$$ -plane has polar coordinates $$r = 2 + \cos 1.5t$$ and $$\theta = t$$ , so $$r$$ varies between $$1$$ and $$3$$ . Then show that $$z$$ has maximum and minimum values when the projection is halfway between $$r = 1$$ and $$r = 3$$ .</paragraph>
        <paragraph>When you have finished your sketch, use a computer to draw the curve with viewpoint directly above and compare with your sketch. Then plot the curve from several other viewpoints. You can get a better impression of the curve if you plot a tube with radius $$0.2$$ around the curve. (Use the tubeplot command in Maple or the tubecurve or Tube command in Mathematica.)</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="61" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Properties of Limits Suppose $$\mathbf{u}$$ and $$\mathbf{v}$$ are vector functions that possess limits as $$\left. t\rightarrow a \right.$$ and let $$c$$ be a constant. Prove the following properties of limits.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\left\lbrack {\mathbf{u}(t) + \mathbf{v}(t)} \right\rbrack = \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{u}(t) + \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{v}(t)$$</item>
          <item>$$\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}c\mathbf{u}(t) = c\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{u}(t)$$</item>
          <item>$$\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\left\lbrack {\mathbf{u}(t) \cdot \mathbf{v}(t)} \right\rbrack = \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{u}(t) \cdot \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{v}(t)$$</item>
          <item>$$\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\left\lbrack {\mathbf{u}(t) \times \mathbf{v}(t)} \right\rbrack = \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{u}(t) \times \lim\limits_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{v}(t)$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="62" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Show that $$\lim_{t\rightarrow a}\mathbf{r}(t) = \mathbf{b}$$ if and only if for every $$\varepsilon &gt; 0$$ there is a number $$\delta &gt; 0$$ such that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\left. \text{if} 0 &lt; \left| {t - a} \right| &lt; \delta \text{then} \middle| {\mathbf{r}(t) - \mathbf{b}} \right| &lt; \varepsilon$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
